  "name": "BETA TRACE PROTEIN (BTP) CSF LEAKAGE",
  "code": "1037",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"173\" data-end=\"544\">The <strong data-start=\"177\" data-end=\"205\">Beta Trace Protein (BTP)</strong> test is used to detect the presence of <strong data-start=\"245\" data-end=\"274\">cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)</strong> in body fluids such as <strong data-start=\"298\" data-end=\"352\">nasal secretions, ear discharge, or wound drainage</strong>.<br data-start=\"353\" data-end=\"356\">BTP is a <strong data-start=\"365\" data-end=\"397\">low molecular weight protein</strong> produced mainly in the <strong data-start=\"421\" data-end=\"447\">central nervous system</strong> and is present in high concentrations in <strong data-start=\"489\" data-end=\"496\">CSF</strong>, but much lower in serum and other body fluids.</p>\n<p data-start=\"546\" data-end=\"588\">This test is clinically significant for:</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"591\" data-end=\"701\">Diagnosing <strong data-start=\"602\" data-end=\"615\">CSF leaks</strong>, especially after <strong data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"649\">head trauma</strong>, <strong data-start=\"651\" data-end=\"673\">skull base surgery</strong>, or <strong data-start=\"678\" data-end=\"699\">spontaneous leaks</strong></li>\n<li data-start=\"704\" data-end=\"790\">Differentiating <strong data-start=\"720\" data-end=\"738\">CSF rhinorrhea</strong> or <strong data-start=\"742\" data-end=\"754\">otorrhea</strong> from regular nasal/ear secretions</li>\n<li data-start=\"793\" data-end=\"865\">Guiding surgical repair and preventing complications like <strong data-start=\"851\" data-end=\"865\">meningitis</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CSF Rhinorrhea</strong></td>\n<td>Leak of CSF into nasal cavity; often due to trauma, surgery, or spontaneous skull base defect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>CSF Otorrhea</strong></td>\n<td>CSF leakage through the ear; may follow skull fractures or temporal bone trauma</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Post-surgical or post-traumatic CSF leak</strong></td>\n<td>Presence of BTP confirms that clear discharge is indeed cerebrospinal fluid</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n<p>A high level of BTP in a fluid sample confirms the presence of CSF, making it more specific and sensitive than older markers like glucose or total protein.</p>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
